WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.120 --> 00:00:07.939
Joss Whedon's Firefly was poised to be the
next huge sci-fi series to change television.

00:00:07.939 --> 00:00:11.460
Unfortunately, those hopes were dashed after
one incomplete season.

00:00:11.460 --> 00:00:14.580
Let's look back at the reasons why Firefly's
lights went out.

00:00:14.580 --> 00:00:17.060
A small, loyal fan base

00:00:17.060 --> 00:00:21.169
Like Joss Whedon's other shows, Buffy the
Vampire Slayer and Angel, Firefly developed

00:00:21.169 --> 00:00:23.070
a strong, dedicated fan base.

00:00:23.070 --> 00:00:27.740
Unfortunately, the fans weren't in great enough
numbers to halt cancellation, despite letter-writing

00:00:27.740 --> 00:00:28.939
campaigns to keep it alive.

00:00:28.939 --> 00:00:32.820
Still, the show's abrupt ending shouldn't
be surprising, considering what happened before

00:00:32.820 --> 00:00:35.289
it even began production...

00:00:35.289 --> 00:00:36.429
Marriage Trouble

00:00:36.429 --> 00:00:40.440
According to Business Insider, Fox's bigwigs
had a problem with two of the characters,

00:00:40.440 --> 00:00:43.010
Zoe and Wash, being happily married.

00:00:43.010 --> 00:00:45.820
Fox told Whedon to nix their marriage, or
they'd nix the deal.

00:00:45.820 --> 00:00:50.100
Whedon revealed in the Firefly DVD commentary
that he stuck to his guns, saying, "Then don't

00:00:50.100 --> 00:00:53.129
pick up the show, because in my show, these
people are married."

00:00:53.129 --> 00:00:56.629
Ultimately, the execs backed down, and the
show made it to the airwaves, but that didn't

00:00:56.629 --> 00:00:58.340
mean the troubles ended.

00:00:58.340 --> 00:01:00.749
Friday Night Fright

00:01:00.749 --> 00:01:05.320
Firefly was scheduled for Friday nights, better
known among TV fans as the "Friday night death

00:01:05.320 --> 00:01:06.320
slot."

00:01:06.320 --> 00:01:11.220
Now, airing on Friday nights doesn't automatically
predestine shows for early cancellation...unless

00:01:11.220 --> 00:01:13.250
that show airs on Fox.

00:01:13.250 --> 00:01:17.290
At over 30 television series, Fox has actually
cancelled more Friday night programs than

00:01:17.290 --> 00:01:18.970
any other network.

00:01:18.970 --> 00:01:21.300
Firefly had a death sentence right out of
the gate.

00:01:21.300 --> 00:01:23.660
The episodes aired out of order

00:01:23.660 --> 00:01:27.780
When Whedon conceived the show, he planned
it as an episodic-yet-continuous story divided

00:01:27.780 --> 00:01:29.630
into hour-long chunks.

00:01:29.630 --> 00:01:33.140
The episodes needed to air in a specific order
to make sense to viewers.

00:01:33.140 --> 00:01:38.000
Somehow, this went over Fox execs' heads,
and each chapter of Firefly aired out of order

00:01:38.000 --> 00:01:39.110
on Fox.

00:01:39.110 --> 00:01:43.131
The pilot—which introduces the characters
and plot of the entire venture—didn't air

00:01:43.131 --> 00:01:45.590
until the end of the show's run.

00:01:45.590 --> 00:01:48.090
The promos didn't capture the spirit of the
show

00:01:48.090 --> 00:01:52.220
Fox's ads, promos, and commercials for Firefly
failed at properly conveying what the show

00:01:52.220 --> 00:01:54.450
was about or what it was in spirit.

00:01:54.450 --> 00:01:58.780
As producer Chris Buchanan said on the DVD
commentary, the campaign "suggested [Firefly]

00:01:58.780 --> 00:02:01.840
was a wacky genre comedy," when it wasn't.

00:02:01.840 --> 00:02:05.870
And as author Amy Pascale wrote in Joss Whedon:
the Biography, viewers who would've loved

00:02:05.870 --> 00:02:10.320
the show were too alienated to watch it, and
those who tuned in because of the promos were

00:02:10.320 --> 00:02:11.320
disappointed.

00:02:11.320 --> 00:02:12.480
Low ratings

00:02:12.480 --> 00:02:15.361
With everything going against it, Firefly's
bad ratings were unsurprising.

00:02:15.361 --> 00:02:18.870
On average, it attracted about 4.7 million
viewers.

00:02:18.870 --> 00:02:23.520
While that's a huge number in and of itself,
it's puny by network television standards.

00:02:23.520 --> 00:02:27.969
According to E! Online, Firefly finished at
98th place in the Nielsen ratings.

00:02:27.969 --> 00:02:31.550
The show itself was excellent, but Fox only
cared about numbers.

00:02:31.550 --> 00:02:32.730
An executive defense

00:02:32.730 --> 00:02:37.010
Despite all the obstacles they placed before
the show's success, the folks at Fox essentially

00:02:37.010 --> 00:02:40.030
played dumb when it came to Firefly's cancellation.

00:02:40.030 --> 00:02:43.480
Former Fox Entertainment president Gail Berman
told the Pittsburgh Post-Gazette, "having

00:02:43.480 --> 00:02:45.920
to cancel it was very difficult."

00:02:45.920 --> 00:02:49.849
Must've been difficult...considering she was
part of the decision-making process that led

00:02:49.849 --> 00:02:53.019
to the series' episodes airing out of order
and on Friday nights.

00:02:53.019 --> 00:02:57.090
At least fans eventually got some closure
in the form of 2005's Serenity, the big motion

00:02:57.090 --> 00:02:58.790
picture followup to the series.

00:02:58.790 --> 00:03:01.930
That's better than some one-season wonders
ever get...

00:03:01.930 --> 00:03:02.930
Thanks for watching!

00:03:02.930 --> 00:03:05.599
Subscribe to our YouTube channel to watch
more videos like the one you just saw.

00:03:05.599 --> 00:03:08.489
And leave us a comment to let us know why
you think we lost Firefly too early...

